public record you know and you know  what I wanted to talk to him about was  more about him and who he was and and as  I pointed out I said what do you got to  lose you know I mean you're already the  epitome of evil I mean I can't do any  more than that to you and I might you  know I might be able to soften some of  that who knows  but my arguments were not persuasive at  the time Noriega was awaiting a sentence  reduction hearing and I had one of my  theories at the time was that he might  be trying to sandbag and wait for that  sentence reduction hearing which  eventually happened and his sentence was  reduced from 40 years to 30 years and at  that sentence reduction hearing  interesting enough the American  ambassador to Panama testified on his  behalf the CIA station chief from Panama  testified on his behalf and the CIA  station chief saying he'd gotten a raw  deal and the Panama that ambassador  testifying that he in fact had done a  great number of things for America and  that he ought to be his sentence ought  to be reduced accordingly the trial  judge reduced the sentence on the basis  of that Noriega as co-conspirators had  gotten such significantly smaller  sentences that his had to be brought  somewhat in line with theirs but you  understand you know if you look at this  from the standpoint of an arrest I mean  as part of the reason I wrote this story  as a cops-and-robbers story first and  foremost was if you look at this from a  standpoint of an arrest it suddenly  looks very different I mean if the  Justice Department sent 20,000 men into  an American city to arrest the fugitive  burned down at one of the city's  neighborhoods in the process killed 400  people at least and had 27 of their own  people killed all to arrest this one guy  you would the questions would be raised  you know what are we doing here and on  top of that once they had him  they then forgave the several life  sentences in about a thousand years  worth of prison sentences on about 15  different individuals to get them to  testify against Noriega at his trial so  there was this calculated decision to go  out and get Noriega and the flip side of  that of course is that by the DEA zone  estimates within a year after the arrest  of Manuel Noriega there was more cocaine  being trans hipped through Panama than  there had been under Noriega rule so  I think you know I always try to tell  the story and let everybody draw their  own conclusions afterwards and this is  true to that pattern but it's all out  there for you guys to figure out what  you think of have this activity let me  take one last question link the general  with mr. Bush when he was running the  CIA well there is the question was if  there any evidence to link and Manuel  Noriega to George Bush when George Bush  was running the Ford administration CIA  and what the evidence we have is Manuel  Noriega wrote in as told to memoir after  he went to prison in which he recounts a  meeting with George Bush in 1976 when  Bush was head of this the CIA and  according to Noriega and worship self  was never made a comment about this and  it consistently said he will not discuss  anything he did the CIA director because  it's a violation of his over the oath he  took when he when he took over the job  but according to Noriega their  discussion concerned the fact that at  the time the Panama Canal treaties were  being negotiated and had hit a sort of  stalled point and Noriega in 1976 was  simply the intelligence chief in in  Panama and Panama was being run by Omar  Torrijos  and the Torrio's and Noriega approached  the CIA station chief in in Managua not  Managua excuse me in Panama City  about what they could do to get this to  get negotiations off the dime and moving  again and the station chief suggested  that something that made it clear how  vulnerable that canal was to potential  terrorism would be a great idea so  according to Noriega under this a group  of panamanians was taken by the CIA to  the United States trained then they  infiltrated the Canal Zone and planted a  couple bombs that were harmless I mean  they were not designed to really blow  anything up they were designed that  to make a show of blowing something up  and the the question that he and Bush  had to talk about was what to you know  how to handle this  now the explosions had happened and they  were being investigated by the Southern  Command and you know Bush wanted to make  sure Noriega wasn't gonna spill the  beans and Noriega according to him a  short Bush that he would not and that  was the one personal contact that  happened between them when Bush ran the  CIA well thank you very much guys buy a  lot of books read them tell your friends  [Applause]  David Harris is the author of shooting  the moon the true story of an American  manhunt unlike any other ever it's  published by Little Brown and company to  find out more visit their website at TW  bookmark com 48 hours of nonfiction  books on TV you're watching book TV on  c-span 2 coming up actress Jane Meadows  widow of entertainers Steve Allen leads  a discussion with their son Bill Allen  about the late talk-show host last book  which challenged popular culture to  raise its standards and after that a  former member of the Nazi youth movement  Friedrich to Bach gives a talk with his  co-author Burnett Rosner Hungarian Jew  and survivor of Auschwitz about their  book an uncommon friendship  next week on book TV an encore booknotes  interview with Harry Stein ethics  columnist for The Wall Street Journal on  his memoir titled how I accidentally  joined the vast right-wing conspiracy  and found inner peace then Lynn Olson  explains her book on the lives of key  women in the civil rights movement  including hi to be wells Ella Baker and  Eleanor Roosevelt and later from this  year's Virginia festival of the book  will show you world war two veterans  describing their books on the conflict  [Music]  why is it more difficult to write a  memoir than a novel next Sunday joined  book TV at the Princeton Club in New  York City for the New Yorker festival  where the private lives panel includes  Frank McCourt Mary Karr Martyn Amos and  Dave Eggers whose memoir a heartbreaking  work of staggering genius has spent 18  weeks on the New York Times nonfiction  paperback bestseller list I don't know  if I could ever write nonfiction in any  way shape or form again having even-even  profile eccentric like in the magazine  article or anything like that which I  used to do have the responsibility is so  grave
 

